$body = <<<'HTML'
<div class="takeaways">
  <p class="takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Ice and water shield is a self-adhered, rubberized-asphalt membrane that seals around nails. In Central Texas it is used for valleys, eaves, low-slope sections, penetrations and wind-driven rain, not ice dams.</li>
    <li>Installed during a roof replacement, it usually adds a modest amount per square of membrane. Installing it as a standalone retrofit costs far more, because shingles have to come off.</li>
    <li>Targeted installation suits most Austin roofs. Full-deck coverage is usually reserved for low slopes, complex roofs and certain metal or tile systems.</li>
    <li>Ask for linear feet, coverage width and product type on the estimate, especially whether a high-temperature membrane is specified under metal.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<p class="lead">Ice and water shield installation cost varies widely, from a few hundred dollars added to a roof replacement to several thousand for full-deck coverage or a retrofit. The price depends on how much membrane goes down, where it goes, which product is used, and whether the roof is already torn off. This guide breaks down each cost driver and explains where self-adhered membrane actually earns its keep on Austin roofs.</p>

<p>First, an Austin-specific point. "Ice and water shield" is a trade name that has become shorthand for a whole category of self-adhered underlayments. In northern states, codes require it at the eaves to stop ice dams. In Central Texas we rarely see ice dams. Here the membrane protects the places where water concentrates or gets pushed sideways: valleys, roof-to-wall junctions, low-slope sections, skylights, chimneys, pipe penetrations, and edges exposed to wind-driven rain from spring thunderstorms.</p>

<h2>Where Self-Adhered Membrane Matters Most on Central Texas Roofs</h2>
<p>To understand the cost, it helps to know why the membrane goes where it does. Standard synthetic underlayment is nailed down and sheds water that runs downhill. It handles normal rain well. Self-adhered membrane bonds directly to the deck and seals around each nail shank, so it keeps out water that is moving the wrong way or pooling. On Austin-area roofs, those conditions show up in predictable places:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Location</th><th>Why water gets in</th><th>How common in Austin</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Valleys</td><td>Two slopes concentrate runoff into one channel, and leaf debris slows drainage</td><td>Very common leak point</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Roof-to-wall and chimney junctions</td><td>Flashing ages and sealants dry out in summer heat</td><td>Very common</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pipe boots, vents, skylights</td><td>Each hole through the deck is a potential path, and rubber boots crack under UV</td><td>Very common</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Low-slope sections (porches, additions)</td><td>Water drains slowly and can back up under shingle laps</td><td>Common on remodeled homes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Eaves and rakes on exposed sides</td><td>Wind-driven rain during spring and summer storms pushes water uphill under shingles</td><td>Situational</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Eaves (ice dams)</td><td>Melting snow refreezes at the cold overhang</td><td>Rare in Central Texas</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Check local code and the shingle manufacturer's instructions as well, because some products and low-slope applications call for self-adhered underlayment regardless of climate.</p>

<h2 id="h-typical-ice-and-water-shield-installation-cost-ranges">Typical Ice and Water Shield Installation Cost Ranges</h2>
<p>The figures below are typical planning ranges from common industry pricing. Local labor rates, product choice and roof complexity all move them.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Typical cost basis</th><th>Planning range</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Added during a roof replacement (targeted areas)</td><td>Per square of membrane installed</td><td>~$100–$250 per square ($1.00–$2.50/sq ft)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>High-temperature membrane under metal</td><td>Per square</td><td>~$150–$300+ per square</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Full-deck coverage during replacement</td><td>Per roof square, replacing standard underlayment</td><td>Adds roughly $75–$200 per square over synthetic underlayment</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Standalone retrofit (remove and reinstall shingles)</td><td>Per job or per linear foot</td><td>Much higher, often more than the membrane itself many times over</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The pattern is simple: membrane is cheap to add when the deck is already exposed and expensive to add later.</p>

<h2 id="h-material-cost-what-the-membrane-itself-costs">Material Cost: What the Membrane Itself Costs</h2>
<p>Self-adhered membranes are sold in rolls that typically cover about 150–225 sq ft (1.5–2.25 squares). Material cost depends on the type:</p>
<div class="feature-grid">
  <div class="feature"><h3>Standard granular-surface</h3><p>The common choice under asphalt shingles. It has a gritty top for traction and is the most budget-friendly option.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Smooth or film-surfaced</h3><p>Often used under metal or where a slick surface helps panels move. Pricing is similar to or slightly above granular.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>High-temperature</h3><p>Rated for the hotter conditions under metal and dark roofs, so the adhesive doesn't soften and run. This matters in Texas heat and costs more per roll.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Synthetic peel-and-stick</h3><p>Lighter, and some versions are designed for long UV exposure during construction. Mid to premium price.</p></div>
</div>
<p>As a rough guide, material alone often lands somewhere around $0.50–$1.50 per sq ft depending on brand and type. Prices change with the market, so treat any number as a planning figure. Our guide to <a href="/blog/roofing-underlayment-material-types/">roofing underlayment material types</a> compares self-adhered membranes with felt and synthetics.</p>

<h2 id="h-labor-cost-why-installation-prices-vary-so-much">Labor Cost: Why Installation Prices Vary So Much</h2>
<p>Laying a straight run of membrane on an open deck is quick. The cost climbs with detail work:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Valleys and crickets</strong> need careful centering and lapping.</li>
  <li><strong>Penetrations</strong> such as vents, skylights and chimneys require cutting, wrapping and sealing.</li>
  <li><strong>Steep pitch</strong> slows every task and needs fall protection.</li>
  <li><strong>Heat.</strong> On a 100°F Austin afternoon the membrane grabs instantly, so crews often schedule membrane work for cooler morning hours to position it correctly.</li>
  <li><strong>Deck preparation.</strong> The membrane needs a clean, dry, sound surface to bond to.</li>
</ul>
<p>Poor workmanship, such as wrinkles, reverse laps or gaps at seams, can channel water instead of shedding it. Installation quality matters as much as the product.</p>

<h2 id="h-targeted-installation-vs-full-coverage">Targeted Installation vs. Full Coverage</h2>

<h3 id="h-targeted-installation">Targeted Installation</h3>
<p>This is the right fit for most Central Texas shingle roofs. Membrane goes where the risk is concentrated:</p>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>Valleys, typically a 36-inch-wide strip centered on the valley</li>
  <li>Around chimneys, skylights and roof-to-wall transitions</li>
  <li>At pipe and vent penetrations</li>
  <li>Along eaves and rakes on exposed elevations prone to wind-driven rain</li>
  <li>Low-slope sections (2/12 to 4/12) where shingles need extra protection</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="h-full-deck-installation">Full-Deck Installation</h3>
<p>Covering the whole deck makes sense in specific cases:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Low-slope roofs near the minimum pitch for the roofing material</li>
  <li>Metal roofs, where some installers and manufacturers prefer a high-temp membrane across the deck</li>
  <li>Tile roofs, where the underlayment does much of the waterproofing</li>
  <li>Very complex roofs with many valleys and dormers</li>
  <li>Homes with a history of leaks from wind-driven rain</li>
</ul>
<p>One caution: full-deck self-adhered membrane is a vapor barrier. On most vented attics this is fine, but it should be paired with proper <a href="/blog/attic-ventilation-upgrade-benefits/">attic ventilation</a> so moisture has a way out.</p>

<h2 id="h-how-roof-size-affects-installation-cost">How Roof Size Affects Installation Cost</h2>
<p>For targeted work, cost follows the linear footage of valleys, edges and penetrations more than overall roof size. A small roof with lots of valleys can need more membrane than a large, simple gable. For full-deck work, cost scales directly with roofing squares. Use the <a href="/roofing-tools/roofing-square-calculator/">roofing square calculator</a> to estimate yours.</p>

<h3 id="h-example-scenario">Example Scenario</h3>
<p>Take a 25-square architectural shingle roof in Pflugerville with four valleys, a chimney and a long roof-to-wall junction at a two-story section:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Measurement</th><th>Membrane</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Valleys (4 × 18 ft, 3 ft wide)</td><td>72 linear ft</td><td>216 sq ft</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Roof-to-wall junction (2 ft wide)</td><td>30 linear ft</td><td>60 sq ft</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Chimney, skylight, pipes</td><td>Allowance</td><td>~75 sq ft</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Low-slope porch tie-in</td><td>1.5 squares</td><td>150 sq ft</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td></td><td><strong>~500 sq ft (5 squares)</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>At a planning rate of $100–$250 per square during replacement, the targeted membrane adds roughly $500–$1,250. Covering the full 25-square deck instead might add about $1,900–$5,000 over standard synthetic underlayment. That gap is why targeted coverage is the usual recommendation for typical Austin shingle roofs.</p>

<h2 id="h-additional-factors-that-increase-cost">Additional Factors That Increase Cost</h2>

<h3 id="h-tear-off-vs-retrofit-installation">Tear-Off vs. Retrofit Installation</h3>
<p>During a full <a href="/services/roof-replacement/">roof replacement</a>, the deck is already bare, so membrane is just one more layer. Adding it to an existing roof means removing shingles in that area, installing the membrane, then reinstalling or replacing the shingles, plus the risk of a poor color match. Retrofits usually only make sense to fix a known leak in a valley or around a chimney.</p>

<h3 id="h-roof-deck-repairs">Roof Deck Repairs</h3>
<p>Self-adhered membrane bonds best to sound, dry plywood or OSB. Rotted or delaminated sheets have to be replaced first, typically priced per 4×8 sheet. Valleys and areas around old chimneys are where we find soft decking most often.</p>

<h3 id="h-drip-edge-and-flashing-upgrades">Drip Edge and Flashing Upgrades</h3>
<p>Membrane works together with metal. At eaves, it typically goes under the drip edge; at rakes, over it. Around chimneys and walls it pairs with step and counter flashing. Replacing old, rusted flashing during the same job adds cost but removes a common leak source.</p>

<h3>Membrane Under Metal and Tile Roofs</h3>
<p>Metal and tile change the math. Under a standing seam or exposed-fastener <a href="/services/metal-roofing/">metal roof</a>, deck temperatures in a Texas summer can soften a standard membrane's adhesive until it oozes or sticks to the panels and restricts their thermal movement. That is why high-temperature membranes are specified for metal in hot climates, and why they cost more per roll. Under concrete or clay tile, the underlayment does much of the long-term waterproofing because water can get past the tiles themselves. Tile roofs often use heavier or self-adhered underlayments over more of the deck, and that raises the membrane line on the estimate.</p>

<h2 id="h-what-to-look-for-in-a-contractor-estimate">What to Look for in a Contractor Estimate</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>Where membrane will be installed (valleys, walls, penetrations, eaves, low-slope areas)</li>
  <li>Width and linear footage, or the squares of membrane</li>
  <li>Product type, and whether it is high-temperature rated when it goes under metal</li>
  <li>How it ties into drip edge and flashing</li>
  <li>Price per sheet for any decking replacement</li>
</ul>
<p>It also helps to ask how the membrane fits with the rest of the system. Is synthetic underlayment going over the remaining deck, and how do the two products lap? Will valleys be open metal or closed-cut shingles over membrane? Are new pipe boots included, or are the old ones being reused? Each answer affects how long the roof stays watertight, and none of them adds much cost when it is planned up front.</p>
<p>A line that just says "ice and water shield as needed" makes it hard to compare bids. Austin Roofing lists the scope clearly in every written quote.</p>

<h2 id="h-is-ice-and-water-shield-worth-the-cost">Is Ice and Water Shield Worth the Cost in Austin?</h2>
<p>For valleys, penetrations, wall junctions and low-slope sections, yes, almost always. The membrane costs little compared with drywall, insulation and ceiling repairs after a leak. Central Texas storms can drive rain sideways under shingles, and live oak debris that collects in valleys holds water against the roof for days. Full-deck coverage is a judgment call based on roof slope, complexity and roofing material. Our <a href="/services/roof-inspection/">roof inspection</a> can identify where your roof would benefit most.</p>

<h2 id="h-final-takeaway">Final Takeaway</h2>
<p>The best value on ice and water shield comes from installing it during a replacement, in the right places, with the right product for Texas heat. Plan for targeted coverage on a typical shingle roof, consider full coverage on low-slope, metal or complex roofs, and make sure the estimate spells out exactly what you are getting. <a href="/contact-us/">Contact Austin Roofing</a> for a free inspection and a detailed quote anywhere in Austin and Central Texas.</p>
